API Server: All nodes need to reach k3s server through port 6443 via TCP
Flannel VXLAN: all sewrver and agent nodes need to be able to reach each other on port 8472 via UDP

by default only root user can read the kubeconfig file gereated by K3s installation.

they are both used, they serve completely different purposes:

6443  → cluster management traffic
        "join cluster, get instructions, report status"
        Agent initiates → Server listens
        Agent → connects TO server on port 6443
8472  → pod network traffic  
        "pod on server talking to pod on agent"
        Both directions, both machines
        Server ↔ Agent communication for Pod networking
API Server (6443/TCP)

This is the control plane communication
How kubectl talks to the cluster
How agents register and get instructions from the server
Think of it as the management channel

Flannel VXLAN (8472/UDP)

This is the Pod-to-Pod network communication
How Pods on different nodes talk to each other
Think of it as the data channel


----

To allow other users to connect to the cluster, we need to allow them to read the kubeconfig file located at /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml

